		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Re: Baofeng DM1701 charger cooling mod]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=5102&amp;p=37161#p37161"><![CDATA[
I’ve been on this forum a few months now because I’ve owned my DM1701, &amp; RT3S for a few months. I feel the OpenGD77 turned my radios from Clark Kent into Superman. I remember years ago the original MD380 chargers would toast the batteries. Here’s my findings. First, I never charge a battery with the radio attached. And I charge my batteries on the porch which does not have A/C, so it could be 90 degrees when I charge. I did order some of the 5100 boards figuring it’s the way to go. But after charging both the 1701, &amp; RT3S on their respective chargers, I measured the battery voltage with my Fluke multimeter just after the charge was complete, &amp; both batteries measured 8.27 volts, well under the 8.4 volt threshold. The wall wart, charging stand, &amp; battery were barely warm even if the porch was 90 F. So seeing that I’m not cooking my batteries, I’m not going to bother using the 5100 boards. Yeah, the chargers take longer than I’m used to with my Motorola Impres chargers, but I’ve got other batteries, or radios to use while my batteries safely, &amp; slowly charge. In closing, had OpenGD77 not existed, I would never have purchased these radios. So my compliments to the gang who created this. GARY<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=8235">N4KVE</a> — Sun May 18, 2025 4:27 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
