		<link href="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=5102&amp;p=36993#p36993"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Baofeng DM1701 charger cooling mod]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=5102&amp;p=36993#p36993"><![CDATA[
DM-1701 lovers,<br>have you noticed an strange smell when you charge your devices on the cradle, that smell of heated/burned pcb, and the bottom of the battery/radio quite warm?<br><br>As I'm pretty curious and wanted to understand what was going on, before having major (and maybe dangerous) failures, I found that the charge controller (HC5812 MSLX401) is heating quite a lot specially when the battery is completely discharged, discovering that the cooling conditions are not the best at all for that component.<br><br>Usually in such cases the use of double sided PCB's with"heat tunnel" is a good practice to avoid sometime to install heat-sinks or having a forced ventilation.<br>Instead, on this cradle the board is a cheap single sided phenolic pcb, that definitely doesn't help at all to keep the IC cool enough to prevent problems on mid-long term.<br><br><img class="smilies" src="http://localhost/opengd77/images/smilies/icon_idea.gif" width="15" height="17" alt=":idea:" title="Idea"> So that's my solution... <div class="inline-attachment"><dl class="file"><dt class="attach-image"><img src="http://localhost/opengd77/download/file.php?id=2943&amp;sid=b4fb7f7884d23447b5ebaf2114dc4a8c" class="postimage" alt="IMG_0312.jpeg" onclick="viewableArea(this);" /></dt></dl></div>On the opposite side of the board I've installed a heatsink (using special heat conductive double sided adhesive), and on the bottom I've placed a simple aluminum plate that dissipate the excess of heat.<div class="inline-attachment"><dl class="file"><dt class="attach-image"><img src="http://localhost/opengd77/download/file.php?id=2945&amp;sid=b4fb7f7884d23447b5ebaf2114dc4a8c" class="postimage" alt="IMG_0315.jpeg" onclick="viewableArea(this);" /></dt></dl></div>The IC is not in direct contact with that plate, using heat transfer silicon mat than ensure an optimal work there.<div class="inline-attachment"><dl class="file"><dt class="attach-image"><img src="http://localhost/opengd77/download/file.php?id=2944&amp;sid=b4fb7f7884d23447b5ebaf2114dc4a8c" class="postimage" alt="IMG_0314.jpeg" onclick="viewableArea(this);" /></dt></dl></div>No more heat...no more smell  <img class="smilies" src="http://localhost/opengd77/images/smilies/icon_e_wink.gif" width="15" height="17" alt=";)" title="Wink"> <br>With that mod now I can also leave the radio powered on the cradle, without risking to face overheating anymore.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=8552">EI8IHB</a> — Sun May 04, 2025 10:00 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
